          <a href="#edges" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Edges, Potholes and Plow Damage</a>
          <a href="#sealing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Should You Seal Now?</a>
          <a href="#deicers" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Deicers and Snow Removal</a>
          <a href="#gravel" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Gravel Driveways</a>
          <a href="#common-mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Common Mistakes to Avoid</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Winter does not damage a driveway. Water does, and winter simply gives it the mechanism — freezing inside cracks and the base, expanding, and prising the surface apart from below over and over for four months.
        </p>

        <div id="checklist" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">1. The Autumn Checklist</h2>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Job</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Priority</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Deadline</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fill every crack</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Highest</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Last mild spell</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Fix drainage and puddles</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Before first freeze</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Repair edges and potholes</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">High</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Before first plow</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Clean surface and joints</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Medium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Before filling</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Place edge markers</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Medium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Before ground freezes</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Stock deicer or sand</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Medium</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Before first storm</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            If the weather turns before you get through all of it, do the cracks. Everything else on this list is worth doing and none of it matters as much.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="inspect" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">2. Clean and Inspect</h2>

          <p>
            Start by clearing the surface: leaves, needles, moss, and the accumulation that collects along edges and in cracks. Leaves left through winter hold moisture against the surface and stain it, and moss is both slippery and a moisture reservoir.
          </p>

          <p>
            A stiff broom and a hose does most of it. A pressure washer is faster and is worth it on a mossy or heavily soiled surface, but let everything dry completely afterwards before filling or sealing anything — a crack filler bonding to a damp crack wall does not stay bonded.
          </p>

          <p>
            Then walk the whole driveway in good daylight with a piece of chalk. Mark every crack, every low spot, every crumbling edge, and every place a joint has opened. Working from marks means you fill what is actually there rather than what you happen to notice while kneeling.
          </p>

          <p>
            Pay attention to what the cracks look like. Isolated straight cracks are surface movement and are straightforward crack-filling work. Interconnected cracking in a pattern resembling alligator hide means the base beneath has failed, and filling it is a holding action rather than a repair — our guide to <a href="/how-to-fix-alligator-cracks-in-asphalt-driveway/">alligator cracking</a> covers what that actually needs.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cracks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">3. Crack Filling Is the Whole Game</h2>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">The Sequence You Are Interrupting</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Open crack in October, water in November, ice in December, a wider crack in January, a pothole in March.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Every freeze widens the void slightly and loosens material around it, and traffic pumps that loosened material out. Nothing else you do in autumn interrupts as much damage per hour spent as closing the cracks before the water gets in.</p>
          </div>

          <p>
            Clean each crack out first. Blow or brush out debris, pull any vegetation with its roots, and remove loose crumbling material from the edges — a screwdriver or a crack-cleaning tool works. Filler bonds to sound material, not to dirt.
          </p>

          <p>
            Match the product to the width. Hairline cracks under about 3 mm generally need a pourable liquid filler. Cracks up to about 12 mm suit a rubberised pourable or a cartridge-applied filler. Wider than that and you are into cold patch or a filler with sand added — our guide to <a href="/how-to-fill-large-cracks-in-asphalt-driveway/">filling large cracks</a> covers that end of the range.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fill slightly proud and allow for settlement, then check back after a day and top up anything that has sunk. Most fillers shrink as they cure, and a crack filled level on Saturday is often slightly hollow by Monday.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mind the temperature. Most cold-pour fillers want above about 10°C to flow and cure, so the practical deadline is the last mild stretch of autumn. If you have missed it and a crack is open, a cold-applied product now still beats leaving it open all winter. Our <a href="/best-driveway-crack-filler/">crack filler testing</a> covers what holds up.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="drainage" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">4. Drainage and Standing Water</h2>

          <p>
            Walk the driveway after rain and note where water sits. Puddles are not cosmetic: a saturated area entering a freeze has vastly more water to expand than a well-drained one, and those are the areas that scale, crack and pothole first.
          </p>

          <p>
            The usual causes are simple and fixable. A downspout discharging onto the surface delivers a roof's worth of water to one spot — extend it past the driveway entirely. A settled low spot needs levelling with a patch or overlay material. Grading that sends runoff from the lawn across the driveway can often be corrected with a shallow swale or a strip of edging.
          </p>

          <p>
            Check the edges too, where a driveway meets lawn or garden. If the surrounding ground has built up above the surface, water is being held against the edge rather than draining off it, and edge deterioration follows. Cutting back the soil to sit slightly below the driveway surface fixes it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clear any drain grates, channels or culverts, and confirm they actually run. A blocked channel drain that sat full through autumn will be a block of ice by January, and a driveway that drains through it has nowhere to send meltwater. Our guide to <a href="/how-to-fix-puddles-in-an-asphalt-driveway/">fixing puddles in a driveway</a> covers the levelling work in detail.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="edges" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">5. Edges, Potholes and Plow Damage</h2>

          <p>
            Asphalt edges are unsupported and are the first thing to crumble, particularly where vehicles turn onto them. A weak edge going into winter is a weak edge with a plow blade coming at it, and blade damage on an already crumbling edge is dramatic.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repair edges before the season with a cold patch material, compacted well, and consider building up the soil alongside to just below surface level so the edge is supported rather than overhanging.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fill potholes properly rather than throwing material in. Remove loose debris and cut back to sound material, apply the patch in layers rather than one deep fill, and compact each layer firmly — a tamper, or the wheel of a car driven back and forth. An uncompacted patch fails in the first freeze. Our <a href="/how-to-fix-crumbling-asphalt-driveway/">crumbling driveway repair guide</a> covers technique.
          </p>

          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Marking for the Plow</h3>
          <p>
            Push driveway markers in along both edges before the ground freezes, because after it does you will be drilling. Set them just outside the surface edge so a blade tracking beside them stays off it, and place extra markers at the entrance, at corners, and beside anything a blade could catch — a drain grate, a raised joint, a settled slab, a septic lid.
          </p>
          <p>
            If a contractor plows for you, walk the driveway with them before the first storm and point out those hazards. It costs ten minutes and prevents an argument in February about who broke what.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="sealing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">6. Should You Seal Now?</h2>

          <p>
            Only if there is genuinely time. Sealer needs surface temperature, air temperature and overnight lows within the product's window, plus a dry cure period afterwards — and the overnight low is the figure that catches people out in autumn, because a mild afternoon can be followed by a night near freezing.
          </p>

          <p>
            A coat applied too late cures poorly, and a poorly cured sealer is worse than no sealer: it can peel, track, and hold moisture against the surface. Our guides to <a href="/what-temperature-to-seal-driveway/">the temperature window for sealing</a> and <a href="/can-you-seal-a-driveway-in-cold-weather/">sealing in cold weather</a> cover the numbers, and <a href="/best-time-to-seal-driveway/">the best time to seal</a> covers the seasonal timing.
          </p>

          <p>
            The practical guidance is this: if you are reading this in early autumn with a stretch of mild, dry weather ahead, sealing is reasonable — fill the cracks first and let them cure, then seal. If you are reading it in late autumn with frost in the forecast, skip the sealing, do the cracks and the drainage, and put sealing on the calendar for next summer.
          </p>

          <p>
            And if the driveway was sealed recently, remember that full cure takes considerably longer than dry-to-touch. Check the product's cure figure before subjecting a fresh coat to a plow blade or a steel shovel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="deicers" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">7. Deicers and Snow Removal</h2>

          <p>
            Buy your deicer or sand before the first storm. The selection after a storm consists of whatever nobody else wanted, which is usually the rock salt you were trying to avoid.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>On concrete</strong>, deicer choice matters a great deal, because chloride salts accelerate freeze-thaw scaling and, on reinforced slabs, corrode the steel. Sand for traction is the safest, calcium magnesium acetate the gentlest melter, and rock salt the one to keep off young or unsealed concrete entirely. We cover the trade-offs in <a href="/how-to-prevent-concrete-spalling-in-winter/">preventing concrete spalling</a>.
          </p>

          <p>
            <strong>On asphalt</strong>, chloride salts are less of a chemical threat because there is no cement paste or reinforcement to attack, but freeze-thaw damage still applies and anything keeping the surface wet feeds it. What genuinely harms asphalt is petroleum-based material, so keep solvents, fuel and oil-based products off it.
          </p>

          <p>
            For clearing, a plastic or poly-edged shovel is kinder to a sealed surface than a steel one. Set snow blower skid shoes so the scraper bar clears the surface slightly rather than riding on it — the settings are in our <a href="/how-to-prepare-a-snow-blower-for-winter/">snow blower pre-season guide</a>. And clear snow rather than letting it melt in place: a driveway under snow for a week is a driveway saturated for a week.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="gravel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">8. Gravel Driveways</h2>

          <p>
            Gravel needs different work but no less of it, and the theme is the same: get the water off before it freezes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Re-grade to restore the crown so water sheds to both sides rather than running down the middle or pooling in ruts. Fill ruts and potholes with fresh material and compact them, because a rut going into winter fills with water, freezes, and becomes a much larger rut by spring.
          </p>

          <p>
            Clean out the ditches and any culvert either side. A blocked culvert in autumn is a solid ice plug in January, and the water it should have carried ends up sitting on or under the driveway instead.
          </p>

          <p>
            Mark the edges thoroughly, because there is no hard boundary for a plow blade to find and the consequence of getting it wrong is your gravel spread across the lawn. Plow with the blade set slightly high for the same reason, and expect to rake gravel back from the edges in spring regardless.
          </p>
        </div>


        <div class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">Booking a Plowing Contractor</h2>

          <p>
            If someone else clears your driveway, book before the season rather than after the first storm, when good contractors are full and the ones still taking calls are the ones nobody kept.
          </p>

          <p>
            Ask three things. Does the price cover a set number of visits or a season, and what triggers a visit — a snowfall depth, or a call from you? Is the crew plowing, blowing or shovelling, since a blade and a blower do very different things to an edge? And who is responsible if something on the property is damaged?
          </p>

          <p>
            Then walk the driveway with them and point at the hazards: the drain grate, the raised joint, the settled slab, the edge you have just repaired, the septic lid. Ten minutes in November prevents an argument in February about who broke what, and most operators genuinely want to know.
          </p>

          <p>
            Agree where the snow goes, too. A pile heaped against the garage door or over a low spot melts back onto the surface and refreezes, and one dumped on the lawn beside the entrance blocks sightlines when you pull out. Snow storage sounds trivial in autumn and matters a great deal by the third storm.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">9. Common Mistakes to Avoid</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Leaving Cracks Open Through Winter</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Water in a crack is the entire spring-pothole mechanism. Filling cracks is the highest-value hour you will spend on a driveway all year, and the deadline is the last mild spell of autumn.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Filling Dirty or Damp Cracks</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Filler bonds to sound clean material. Blow out debris, pull vegetation with roots, remove loose edges, and let everything dry before filling — otherwise the filler lifts out on the first freeze.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Sealing Too Late in the Season</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Overnight lows are what catch people out. A sealer that cannot cure properly peels and tracks and holds moisture against the surface. Late autumn is for crack filling and drainage, not for sealing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Not Marking the Edges Until It Snows</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">Markers go in before the ground freezes, not after the first storm when you are trying to push them into frozen soil in the dark. Add extras beside drains, joints and anything a blade could catch.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Dumping Cold Patch Without Compacting</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">An uncompacted pothole patch fails in the first freeze. Cut back to sound material, fill in layers, and compact each layer firmly with a tamper or by driving over it repeatedly.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying Deicer After the First Storm</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">What is left on the shelf is rock salt, which is the product most likely to scale concrete. Buy a concrete-safe melter or a bag of sand in autumn while there is still a choice.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">10. Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should you seal before winter?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Only with time to cure in the right conditions, which usually means finishing by early autumn. Overnight lows are the catch. If you can only do one job, fill the cracks — it matters far more than sealing.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">When is it too late to fill cracks?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Most cold-pour fillers need above about 10°C to flow and cure, so the last mild autumn spell is the practical deadline. If you have missed it, a cold-applied product still beats leaving a crack open all winter.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do you prevent plow damage?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Mark the edges before the ground freezes, repair crumbling edges first, and point out hazards — drain grates, raised joints, settled slabs — to whoever plows. A blade catching a weak edge takes a chunk with it.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What ice melt is safe on asphalt?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Chlorides are less of a chemical threat to asphalt than to concrete, but freeze-thaw damage still applies. Sand is safest, calcium magnesium acetate the gentlest melter. Keep petroleum-based products off asphalt entirely.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you plow a newly sealed driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Once the sealer has fully cured, which takes much longer than dry-to-touch — check the product figure. Use a plastic-edged shovel rather than steel, and set plow or snow blower clearance so nothing scrapes the surface.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Why do potholes appear in spring?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Water got into an autumn crack, froze and expanded all winter, and progressively broke up the material around it while traffic pumped the loose material out. Autumn crack filling is what interrupts that sequence.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does gravel need preparation?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Yes, mostly grading. Fill ruts, restore the crown so water sheds, clear ditches and culverts, and mark edges clearly since a blade has no hard boundary to find. Plow with the blade set slightly high.</p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is pressure washing worth it?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">Cleaning is, though a brush and hose often suffice. Leaves stain and hold moisture, moss is slippery and retains water, and debris in cracks stops filler bonding. If you pressure wash, let it dry fully before filling or sealing.</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

