        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            <strong class="text-white">Fill the cracks.</strong> If autumn gets away from you and you only manage one job, make it that one — every spring pothole started as an open crack that took on water in November.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Then get the water off the surface: extend downspouts past the driveway, level the low spots, clear the drains, and cut back soil that holds water against the edges. Repair crumbling edges and potholes before a plow blade finds them.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Mark the edges before the ground freezes, buy a concrete-safe deicer or a bag of sand while there is still a choice on the shelf, and leave sealing for next summer unless you genuinely have a mild dry stretch to cure it in.
